  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...\n\nSunny and mostly clear skies will continue through the remainder of \nthe afternoon and overnight as the high pressure remains overhead. \nThis along with calming winds will make for favorable radiational \ncooling conditions dropping tonight's lows considerably cooler into \nthe upper 30s in the Adirondacks and the upper 40s in the mid Hudson \nValley. Patchy/areas of fog will occur in the typical sheltered \nvalleys.\n\nTomorrow will remain dry, with increasing cloud cover as the high \nbegins to depart towards the New England coast with a warmer \nsoutherly flow developing. This will bring a low pressure system up \nthe coast from the south, coinciding with a short wave and surface \ncyclone near the NYC/Long Island area. Along with near normal to \nslightly above normal temperatures tomorrow, increased moisture will \nbe advected into the region with ensemble models showing PWAT \nanomalies increasing to +1 to +2 STDEV Saturday afternoon to near \nthe climatological 75th percentile for the region. Deep warm cloud \nlayers are favorable for efficient warm rain processes but LCL-EL \nmean winds look fairly quicker at about 20-25 kt limiting concerns \nof repeated heavy showers over one location. CAMs have the timing of \nshowers beginning around sunset and overspreading the region with \nmore steadier showers after about 10 PM EDT and continuing through \nearly Sunday morning before tapering off mid morning. Rainfall \ntotals are expected to be in the 0.75\" to 1.00\" for areas south and \neast of the Capital Region and 0.25\" to 0.50\" for areas north and \nwest of the Capital Region. Localized higher end amounts up to \n1.50\" are also possible especially in terrain enhanced areas. \nProbabilistic guidance has a widespread 60% to 70% chance for \nat least 0.25\" however only a 20% to 30% chance for receiving \n1.00\" or greater. With currently dry antecedent soil conditions \nand FFG being 2.5\" to 3.0\"+, this looks to be more of a \nbeneficial rainfall with a low chance for hydro concerns. \nHowever, ponding of water or nuisance flooding could occur in \ntypical low lying, urban, or poor drainage areas. Instability is\nalso lacking for any severe chances Saturday afternoon.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2... \n\nAfter a brief break Sunday morning, isolated to widely scattered \nthunderstorms are expected to develop Sun afternoon ahead of a \npotent upper level short wave and associated surface cold front. \nGuidance indicating moderate instability developing, with 1000-1500 \nJ/Kg SBCAPE across eastern NY, with < 1000 J/Kg in western New \nEngland. 0-6 km shear also increases to around 40-50 kt and moderate \n700-500mb lapse rates range from 6-7 C/km. The CAPE/shear balance \nmay result in organized linear storms, with some becoming strong to \nsevere. Current CAMs show the best chance to be areas north and west \nof the Capital Region. With any thunderstorms that develop, damaging \nwinds will be the primary threat and could blow around unsecured \nobjects and down small tree limbs. Some uncertainty still exists in \nthe timing of the cold front with a potential limiting factor being \nthe amount of daylight left to help initiate storms when all these \ningredients come together. The threat for storms will end Sun night, \nas a drier and cooler air mass builds in by Mon morning.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 3... \n\nHigh pressure begins to build back in on Monday with a dry start to \nnext week. Temperatures look to be seasonable to sightly above \nnormal with the next best chance for showers arriving mid to late \nweek as the next frontal system approaches from the Great Lakes.",
